venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# *.joblib is deliberately NOT ignored: model_d3_price.joblib is D3's trained
# GradientBoostingRegressor and the pricing engine blends it into every
# cold-start cell's target price. Ignoring it meant a fresh clone silently
# priced 44.6% of the grid off a peer median instead of the model.


# --- secrets: never commit. See .env.example for the shape. ---------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets.toml
.streamlit/secrets.toml
*.key
*.pem

# Per-clone LLM spend ledger. The CACHE is committed on purpose (a shared
# cache makes every teammate's rehearsal free); the ledger is local state and
# would conflict on every pull.
eda_output/llm_spend.json
# Append-only, per clone, and it records a machine rather than the
# code -- four clones appending to one tracked file would conflict on
# every line. Same reasoning as the ledger above.
eda_output/run_journal.jsonl
# Live rep feedback: append-only per clone, same conflict-on-every-line
# shape as the journal above. The committed artefact is the SAMPLE
# (eda_output/rep_feedback.sample.jsonl); this is whatever a given clone
# has recorded, and `feedback_capture.py --seed` regenerates it.
eda_output/rep_feedback.jsonl

# --- D6 app (added with the app handover) ---------------------------------
# `venv/` above does NOT match `.venv/`, and the app's own virtual environment
# is ~662 MB. Without this line one careless `git add -A` ships all of it.
.venv/
env/
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
# The app's SQLite database is CREATED ON FIRST RUN and holds uploaded briefs
# as BLOBs, so it is machine-local, grows with use, and would conflict on every
# pull. The schema is app/db/schema.sql, which IS committed -- that is the part
# the app actually needs; the database rebuilds itself from it.
app_data/

# --- hackathon-supplied data and derived artefacts: never commit ----------
Urban Media Datasets/
eda_output/
*.csv
*.docx
*.parquet
*.joblib
*.mp4
